113	A function declaration consists of a name, a sequence of type parameters enclosed in white square brackets 
114	with associated bounds in curly braces, a type for the domain of the function, and the return type of the function. 
115	In the example\\
116	
117	$\begin{array}{l}
118	{\it f}\ob{X<: \{M\}, Y<:\{N\}}({\tt List}\ob{X}, {\tt Tree}\ob{Y}) : {\tt Map}\ob{X,Y} \\
119	\end{array}$\\
120	
121	\noindent $f$ is the name of the function, $X$ and $Y$ are declared type parameters bounded
122	by types $M$ and $N$ respectively, the tuple type $({\tt List}\ob{X},{\tt Tree}\ob{Y})$
123	is the domain of the function, and $ {\tt Map}\ob{X,Y}$ is its return type.  To reduce clutter, we omit
124	the white square braces when there are no type parameters and the curly braces for singleton bounds.
